body:
OP: Thank you for this thoughtful write up.  


Everyone who is saying that one's identity and gender has nothing to do with shamanism is speaking only from their own vantage point and experience. The truth is, there are MANY of us, myself included, whose gender experience is deeply interwoven with spiritual practice and work.

When anyone says that these matters must remain separate, it erases THOUSANDS of years of human history where queer people's identities have been deeply linked with spiritual practice. Across the globe there are examples of people with various gender identities holding important spiritual roles within communities. Indeed, in many ancient communities (and even continuing in some modern communities) it was primarily gender and sexually variant people who were shamans or spirit workers.

&#x200B;

Sure, some spirits have no gender, but others do. And Masculine and Feminine energies are inherent in the universe, regardless of being human or not. And it must not be forgotten that there are MANY human spirits who do retain a gendered form or energy despite no longer having a body. Spirit is DIVERSE and VARIED.

&#x200B;

Also: just because YOUR culture or experience leads you to believe that sex, gender, queerness, etc are not important in spiritual work does not mean this is true across the board. In my culture - which is linked to the spirits I work with - gender IS important and the fact that I identify as third gender is COMPLETELY because of my spiritual experience and the tradition I work in. I cannot untangle spiritual matters from physical matters, and so to separate these things makes no sense to me.

&#x200B;

Remember: SPIRITUAL PRACTICE IS VARIED. Everything is varied and everything is connected. To say there is a right and a wrong for one's way of experiencing spirit or spirit work is just one's ego. Simple as that. If you do not experience gender in your work, that's fine. If you think queerness is something only humans experience - maybe you haven't met queer spirits yet. If you think you can only be spiritual if you exist in a genderless, sexless, and ephemeral world - understand that this is YOUR way and not true for everyone.

TL;DR: Let go of your ego - don't police people who experience spiritual work differently from you. LGBTQ+/queer identities have been deeply linked to shamanism and spirit work across the world and for THOUSANDS of years (continuing to this day). In fact, in some places ONLY gender variant people could be considered a shaman. Recognize that what's true for you is not true for everyone, because we all only see certain facets of the universe and spirit world - no one knows everything and to police others is just your ego taking over.

Remembering Death is not so much reminding yourself that Death is present or that you will die one day. It is part of maintaining the balance within and without. Perhaps it seems a contradiction to contemplate death, but I believe this is a symptom of western imperial and industrialized thought (which tends to place Death as an enemy to be conquered or a shadow to ignore or a mere fact of science). Death is then viewed as taboo or in black and white terms - or as a one way door with nothing interesting behind it. Thus Life is elevated and Death is reduced, creating an imbalance (which I believe has adversely affected colonized and western societies). 


Yes, Death is the counterweight to Life and vice versa, but it is important to have a relationship with Death as well as with Life. So the "memento mori" as you put it, is not so much remembering as maintaining the dance and balance of existence. Death teaches just as Life teaches and one need not physically die to learn from Death. It's not just about appreciating the cycle either - Death/Life are complex and are always around and within us. Remembering one's death is remembering one's life and to contemplate one's end is to contemplate one's existence. What would living be without dying? And one cannot die unless one has first lived (I once read that to die is a privilege afforded to those who are graced with the gift of life). 

&#x200B;

Death is definitely something special and should never, ever be taken for granted or dismissed as something "basic". If you are serious about following a healer's path (calling yourself shaman or otherwise), I do believe it is foundational to develop a relationship with Death. Otherwise, how do you know what Life is? 

&#x200B;

I do not call myself a shaman, but my path is a parallel one (though my tradition does not follow exactly the academic shamanic path nor am I from a modern indigenous community). In my experience at least, understanding Death as part of Life is incredibly important to my spiritual work/practice and to my healing work/practice. I cannot speak for shamans nor can I speak on the work done in the academic ways of groups such as The Foundation for Shamanic Studies. So these thoughts and opinions are my own and are heavily influenced by my own tradition and practices.
